CAT = "abgForce"; SEG = "FOR"
Q = [
("A shoplifter you lawfully arrested stops struggling and sits down quietly. Under section 25 you should:",
 "Stop using force", ["Keep a firm hold", "Apply a wrist lock", "Push them to the floor"],
 "Section 25 justifies only as much force as is necessary. Once the person stops resisting, no further force is needed, so it must stop."),
("A guard who uses more force than the situation needed can be:",
 "Criminally liable for it", ["Protected by their licence", "Excused during an arrest", "Covered by the employer"],
 "Section 26 says everyone authorized to use force is criminally responsible for any excess. The guard may also be sued or dismissed."),
("Force likely to cause death or grievous bodily harm is justified only when you believe on reasonable grounds it is needed to:",
 "Save a life from grave harm", ["Recover stolen merchandise", "Stop a fleeing trespasser", "End a shouted threat"],
 "Section 25(3) bars such force unless it is necessary for the self-preservation of the person, or of anyone under their protection, from death or grievous bodily harm."),
("You see a man about to throw a brick at another person's head. Section 27 lets you:",
 "Use reasonable force", ["Only shout a warning", "Use any force at all", "Do nothing until police come"],
 "Section 27 justifies as much force as is reasonably necessary to prevent an offence likely to cause immediate and serious injury to anyone."),
("In deciding whether force used in self-defence was reasonable, section 34 has the court consider:",
 "The parties' size and age", ["The guard's years of service", "The wishes of the client", "The value of the site"],
 "Section 34(2) lists factors including the nature of the threat, other means to respond, any weapon, and the size, age, gender and physical capabilities of the parties."),
("Section 34 can apply when you believe on reasonable grounds that:",
 "You face force or a threat", ["A site rule is being broken", "Someone is being rude to you", "Property may be lost later on"],
 "Self-defence under section 34 needs a reasonable belief that force is being used or threatened against you or another person, and a reasonable response to it."),
("Under section 35, an act done to remove a trespasser from property you guard must be:",
 "Reasonable in the situation", ["Approved by police first", "Done within one minute", "Witnessed by the client"],
 "Section 35 protects a person acting for whoever is in peaceable possession of property only when the act is reasonable in the circumstances."),
("Section 35 does not protect force used against a person who is:",
 "Lawfully enforcing the law", ["Trespassing after dark", "Damaging the property", "Taking property away"],
 "Section 35(3) says the defence does not apply against someone doing what they are required or authorized by law to do in enforcing the law, unless you reasonably believe they are acting unlawfully."),
("The course says what share of situations can be handled without using force?",
 "About 80%", ["About 20%", "About 50%", "About 99%"],
 "The manual says it is commonly said that about 80% of situations can be handled without force, through verbal and non-verbal communication."),
("If your calm words are already settling a person down, the use of force decision model says to:",
 "Carry on without force", ["Add a firm hold anyway", "Get your baton ready", "Move to a restraint"],
 "The model asks first whether verbal and non-verbal tactics are resolving it. If yes, carry on without force."),
("Before choosing to use force, the course asks you to weigh your own:",
 "Fitness, fatigue, skills", ["Seniority and pay level", "Time the shift started", "Condition of your uniform"],
 "Your age, fitness, strength, injuries, tiredness, skills and confidence all decide whether you can use force safely."),
("Icy ground, poor lighting and nearby bystanders are which use of force factor?",
 "Environment", ["The subject", "Your skill set", "The urgency"],
 "The situation factors include threats to others, urgency and the environment: lighting, weather, surface and bystanders."),
("A police officer asks you to help separate two fighting fans. Section 25 covers your force when you act:",
 "In aid of a peace officer", ["As a peace officer", "Under the client's rules", "As a bylaw officer"],
 "Section 25(1)(c) covers a person acting in aid of a peace officer, on reasonable grounds, using as much force as is necessary."),
("A small, frightened shoplifter tries to walk away after your lawful arrest. Reasonable force is most likely:",
 "A hold on the wrist", ["A tackle to the ground", "A strike to the legs", "A choke hold"],
 "Reasonable force matches the subject and the situation. A fearful person of small build can be stopped with a hold on the wrist or elbow."),
("If you must hold a restrained person until police arrive, the course advises you to:",
 "Sit them up, chest free", ["Lay them face down", "Kneel across their back", "Bend them over a desk"],
 "Pressure on the chest or a blocked mouth and nose can stop a restrained person breathing. Seat them or prop them against a wall."),
("Your employer must keep its records of incidents where a guard used force for at least:",
 "2 years", ["6 months", "1 year", "5 years"],
 "Section 12(5) of the Security Services and Investigators Regulation requires records of incidents involving material use of force to be kept for a minimum of 2 years."),
("After you use force on duty, you must be ready to:",
 "Justify and record it", ["Keep it off the record", "Tell only the client", "Wait to be asked"],
 "Using force is a serious matter. Record the events, and be ready to justify your actions and answer questions about them."),
("The basic course does not teach use of force techniques. It recommends that you:",
 "Take a force course", ["Learn it on the job", "Watch police videos", "Ask the site client"],
 "Use of force techniques are not covered; the manual recommends an appropriate course on use of force and personal safety."),
("In a crowd that is panicking to get out, threats and force from security will:",
 "Increase the fear", ["Restore order fast", "Slow the stampede", "Calm the leaders"],
 "Threats and force only add to fear and panic. Speak confidently and loudly enough to be heard, without yelling."),
("Even if no charge is laid, a guard who uses excessive force may still face:",
 "A lawsuit or firing", ["No other consequence", "Only a verbal warning", "Only a demerit point"],
 "Beyond criminal responsibility, excessive force can lead to discipline or dismissal by the employer and a civil suit."),
]
